# Lab 8 – Bias-Variance Tradeoff and Learning Curves

## Objective

The objective of this lab was to understand the bias-variance tradeoff by exploring how model complexity affects performance. I learned to recognize underfitting and overfitting and used learning curves to evaluate how well models generalize to new data.

## What I Did

In this lab, I created polynomial regression models with different degrees to compare simple, well-balanced, and overly complex models. I visualized how each model fit the data and identified examples of underfitting, good model fit, and overfitting. I also generated learning curves to compare training and validation performance, helping diagnose whether a model suffered from high bias or high variance.

## What I Learned

This lab showed me that selecting the right model complexity is essential for building accurate machine learning models. I learned how underfitting occurs when a model is too simple, while overfitting occurs when a model memorizes the training data instead of learning general patterns. Learning curves provided a valuable way to evaluate model performance and determine whether a model could benefit from more data or a different level of complexity.

